        "signature": "",
        "thinking": "Now I can see the sources of the `http` imports:\n\n1. **`@smithy/node-http-handler`** - Part of AWS SDK, used for making HTTP requests in Node.js. This is bundled because the S3 storage provider uses the AWS SDK.\n\n2. **`@smithy/credential-provider-imds`** - AWS credential provider that makes HTTP requests to IMDS. Also from S3 storage.\n\n3. **Line 84687 - `createServer from \"node:http\"`** - This is INSIDE A STRING! It's in `getSandboxCallbackBridgeServerSource()` which returns a source code string as a string literal. \n\nThe real culprits are the AWS SDK packages pulling in Node's `http` and `https` modules, which won't work in Cloudflare. I should mark either the AWS SDK packages or the Node built-ins as external to prevent them from being bundled. , but if I mark `http` as external, it won't be bundled... but then it will try to be resolved at runtime by the CF runtime, which doesn't have it. That's the current situation.\n\nHmm, let me think. I need these `http` imports to NOT fail at runtime. Options:\n\n1. Mark `@aws-sdk/*` and `@smithy/*` as external - the S3 storage code won't work at runtime in CF (R2 is used anyway), but at least the bundle won't have `http` imports.\n\n2. Provide a stub for `http` that has empty implementations - the S3 code will import `http` and get the stub. The S3 code won't work (but it's not called in CF anyway).\n\nActually, marking those packages as external won't work either—the Worker will still fail when the CF runtime tries to resolve them at load time, even if those code paths never execute. The real solution is to create stub modules for all the Node.js APIs that CF Workers don't support—`http`, `https`, `net`, `tls`, `fs`, and the rest—so they get bundled as empty implementations instead of failing to resolve. I'm realizing the issue with marking AWS packages as external—the Worker bundle would still try to import them, which won't work in Cloudflare's environment. The cleaner approach is to stub out the Node.js built-ins that AWS SDK depends on, so imports succeed but the APIs either fail gracefully or throw meaningful errors when actually invoked.",
        "type": "thinking"
      }
    ],
